The duplicate-detection engine in this tool is an independent implementation.
Its design approach references BookOrbit's duplicate module
(https://github.com/bookorbit/bookorbit, AGPL-3.0-only with §7 additional terms).
The boundary of what was borrowed versus written here, stated plainly:

Borrowed design (method/structure level, not protectable expression):
  · two-tier evidence ranking (exact ISBN stronger than fuzzy title match)
  · match reasons expressed as data (enum), rendered by the UI per locale
  · comparison showing only fields that differ between copies
  · keeper selection by tuple comparison (no ambiguous "tie score")
  · dismissals that survive a rescan

The media-family format lists (three frozensets in dedup/normalize.py) match
BookOrbit's `mediaFamilyForFormat` in book-duplicate-normalize.ts: the six
audiobook and four comic entries are identical, in the same order, and the ebook
list is its superset (+txt/docx — txt comes from the MyBooks host, docx is a
Calibre input format). **This list was taken from BookOrbit, not derived from
the host**: MyBooks' ingest formats (BaseTool.SUPPORTED_FORMATS = {epub, pdf,
azw3, mobi, txt}) contain no audiobook or comic formats to derive it from. The
justification: a short "filename extension → media type" table is factual,
functional expression — ideas and facts are not copyrightable, and where
function essentially exhausts the available options the merger doctrine
applies. Reproducing it does not make a derivative work.

What was NOT copied:
  · no code of any kind; everything here was written from scratch
    (backend/dedup/ and backend/driver.py)
  · no copy of its FEATURE_WEIGHTS table, STOPWORDS list, or provider identifier maps
  · field sets, weights and thresholds are this repository's own, fitted to
    MyBooks/Calibre data
  · Chinese text handling (character bigrams, variant scoring, trailing-annotation
    stripping) is this repository's own design; BookOrbit has no equivalent

Conclusion: this tool contains no protectable expression from BookOrbit — no code
was copied, and the only overlap is the unprotectable functional fact list above —
so it is **not a derivative work and carries no AGPL obligation from BookOrbit**. The LICENSE here
is this tool's own (AGPL-3.0; versions before 2026-10-03 were BSD-2-Clause).
